    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain how ace inhibitors like lisinopril actually work? specifically the raas pathway part. i need to understand how they lower blood pressure for my exam.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A Levitra inhibitor, like lisinopril, works by blocking the angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) in the lungs, which is a key component of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS). When ACE inhibitors bind to ACE, they prevent the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, a potent vasoconstrictor that raises blood pressure. By blocking this conversion, ACE inhibitors cause blood vessels to dilate, reducing blood pressure and decreasing the workload on the heart. This prevents too much fluid retention and triggers a response to lower blood pressure.",
